On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:01:01 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> Git (git.git) keeps its state in the list. Junio posts "What's cooking
>> in git.git" regularly, and it gives the state of every topic: "Will
>> merge to next", "Expecting a reroll", "Will discard", and so on.
>> Every state comes from one maintainer message.
>
> It gives the state of every topic? How does that work? Also it seems to
> be branch-based, rather than patch-based.

I overstated it. The report covers only topics in flight, and you are
right that it is branch-based. When Junio picks up a series he applies
it to a topic branch named for the author, e.g. rs/archive-zip-..., and
merges that into seen. A reroll replaces the branch until the topic
reaches next; after that it changes only by incremental patches.

The report is generated by the Meta/cook script on his todo branch. It
walks the topic merges in master..seen, so a topic appears once it is
merged to seen and leaves when it graduates to master or is discarded.
maintain-git.adoc says a topic inactive for three weeks may be dropped
from seen, and SubmittingPatches says one with unaddressed review may
go after four weeks. Each entry carries a "source: <message-id>" line,
which the script recovers from notes that git am leaves on each commit.
Here is an entry from the current issue:

    * rs/archive-zip-omit-out-of-range-mtime (2026-10-12) 2 commits
     - SQUASH??? if time_t is 64 bit ...
     - archive-zip: omit UT extra field if mtime is out of range
     ...
     Expecting a reroll.
     cf. <3a7868a2-a65d-4540-8ab1-70cfbae9754f@web.de>
     source: <595dafac-33c7-4c45-9cac-c868cf1f8bb0@web.de>

So the link runs from commit to mail, never from mail to state.

> I can't imagine that git reports on every single email thread that has
> ever existed, that would be insane.

It does not. A thread Junio never applies never appears anywhere, and
an old version drops out because its branch was replaced. Git cuts the
noise by never tracking most mail. That does not carry over to us,
because we read the list itself in notmuch, where every thread stays
visible until something marks it.

> The question I'm trying to address here is, how do I reduce the noise
> from old patches? The first set of patches is superseded ones. Then I
> also need a way to archive / reject some.
>
> Previously I was going to use folders, but now that I'm planning to use
> real email, I need to find a different approach.

Tags derived from the mail can do the work folders did. A thread is
tagged from what the list says about it, and search.exclude_tags hides
the tag from every search that does not name it. The thread stays where
it is, so nothing has to be moved. Supersession is posted as a series,
below. Rejection and archiving can take the same shape once you settle
how approved and rejected are written, which you asked about in your
follow-up [1]. I will answer that there.

>> sourcehut (lists.sr.ht) is the closest match to what you propose. A
>> reply in a patchset thread can carry the header
>> "X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update:" with one of PROPOSED, NEEDS_REVISION,
>> SUPERSEDED, APPROVED, REJECTED or APPLIED. The list sets the
>> patchset's state from it.
>
> Interesting can you provide docs and examples for this? Also how does it
> compare to other methods?

The docs are one paragraph under "Email controls":

    https://man.sr.ht/lists.sr.ht/#email-controls

    Email replies to patchset threads can include a custom header,
    X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update to update the status of the patchset
    to PROPOSED, NEEDS_REVISION, SUPERSEDED, APPROVED, REJECTED, or
    APPLIED.

They give no example. In use it is one added header on an ordinary
reply:

    In-Reply-To: <cover.123.git.someone@example.org>
    X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update: APPROVED

    Looks good, thanks.

The docs do not say who may set it, so I read the source
(git.sr.ht/~sircmpwn/lists.sr.ht, api/lists/patchset.go and
archive.go). A list moderator may set any state. The submitter, matched
by From, may set only REJECTED, SUPERSEDED or NEEDS_REVISION, and only
while the patchset is not yet applied, approved or rejected. A header
that fails these checks is logged and ignored, and no bounce goes out.
The web UI and "hut lists patchset update" set the same state.

lists.sr.ht also supersedes on its own. When a new version arrives with
the same subject and submitter and a higher version number, the
previous one is marked SUPERSEDED if it was still PROPOSED or
NEEDS_REVISION.

Compared with the others:

- lists.sr.ht: the state lives in its database. Mail is one way to
  change it, and the headers stay in the archive, but the permission
  checks and ordering live in the server.
- Patchwork: the state lives in its own database, set in the web UI,
  through the API or pwclient, or by a git hook that matches pushed
  commits to patches by diff hash. Core Patchwork has a Superseded
  state but does not set it by itself; kernel.org runs separate bots
  for that.
- b4 and lore: everything is mail. b4 writes the "Changes in vN" and
  "Link to vN" lines and a change-id, and "b4 ty" sends "Applied,
  thanks!". There is no tracker; b4 keeps its thank-you bookkeeping on
  the maintainer's machine.
- git.git: the state lives in the maintainer's branches, and the
  report is generated from them and mailed out.

We would be closest to b4 and lore, with a sourcehut-style control
written as a body trailer rather than a header.

> I don't mind projecting state in another tool that makes it all more
> visible... but it should be possible to reconstruct it from the mailing
> list and/or git repo.
>
> Essentially there needs to be a database somewhere. That can be the
> corpus of emails, metadata in a git branch, or a dedicated database.

With this approach the database is the corpus. The notmuch index is a
cache over it: mail-tag.sh recomputes every derived tag on each notmuch
new, so deleting the index and running notmuch new brings the same
state back. Any other view, such as a report like What's cooking or a
web page, can be generated from notmuch queries and needs no state of
its own.

